Presidential elections were held in [[Haiti]] on 25 October 2015, alongside local elections and the second round of the [[2015 Haitian parliamentary election|legislative elections]].<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-latin-america-31864759 Haiti sets date for long overdue elections] BBC News, 13 March 2015</ref> Incumbent [[President of Haiti|President]] [[Michel Martelly]] was [[Constitution of Haiti|constitutionally]] barred from running. As no candidate received a majority of the vote in the first round, a runoff was to be held on 27 December 2015.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-election-idUSKCN0SU36N20151105 Haiti's government candidate advances to presidential runoff] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230131001101/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-election-idUSKCN0SU36N20151105 |date=31 January 2023 }} Reuters, 5 November 2015</ref> On 22 December the [[Conseil Electoral Provisoire]] (CEP) announced that the runoff has been postponed indefinitely.<ref name=postpone2>{{cite news |last=Staff |first=Sentinel |url=http://sentinel.ht/2015/12/22/december-27-elections-indefinitely-postponed/ |title=Haiti Elections indefinitely postponed |work=Haiti Sentinel |location=Miami |publisher=Haiti Sentinel |date=2015-12-22 |access-date=2015-12-27 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151225052220/http://sentinel.ht/2015/12/22/december-27-elections-indefinitely-postponed/ |archive-date=2015-12-25 |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, on 1 January 2016 President [[Michel Martelly]] announced that the runoff would be held on 17 January,<ref name=postpone>{{cite web |url=http://bigstory.ap.org/article/0a3c1dd615fa4a7d977b8337436b7902/haiti-president-says-postponed-runoff-vote-slated-jan-17 |title=Haiti president says postponed runoff vote slated for Jan 17 |agency=Associated Press |date=1 January 2016 |access-date=1 January 2016 |archive-date=5 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160105054558/http://bigstory.ap.org/article/0a3c1dd615fa4a7d977b8337436b7902/haiti-president-says-postponed-runoff-vote-slated-jan-17 |url-status=dead }}</ref> but on 7 January the President changed the date to 24 January. On 20 January, [[Jude Célestin]] issued a statement that calls "whatever the person who will participate in this January 24 [runoff], is a traitor to the Nation".<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-16356-haiti-flash-message-to-the-nation-of-jude-celestin.html |title=aiti - FLASH : Message to the Nation of Jude Célestin |publisher=Haiti Libre |date=2016-01-18 |access-date=2016-01-20 }}</ref> Because of rioting and electoral violence, on 22 January the CEP decided to postpone the second round again, with no specific date given,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-16394-haiti-flash-elections-of-january-24-are-canceled.html |title=Haiti - FLASH : Elections of January 24 are canceled ! |work=HaitiLibre |date=22 January 2016 |access-date=22 January 2016}}</ref> even after President Michel Martelly confirmed the previous day in a nationwide speech that the election should still take place.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-16384-haiti-elections-there-will-be-elections-this-january-24-dixit-michel-martelly.html |title=Haiti - Elections : "There will be elections this January 24" dixit Michel Martelly |work=HaitiLibre |date=21 January 2016 |access-date=22 January 2016}}</ref> The run-off date was later agreed to take place on 24 April 2016.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.plenglish.com/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=4573131&Itemid=1|title = Prensa Latina - Latin American News Agency}}</ref> 

After the preliminary results were published on 25 October 2015, Jude Célestin said he did not recognize them. His criticism was joined by five other presidential candidates. They issued a joint statement denouncing the results as "anti-democratic" and called for the people's vote to be respected. Martelly openly declared his support for Moïse.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.jacobinmag.com/2016/05/haiti-elections-hillary-clinton-fraud-corruption-earthquake-martelly/|title=Clinton's Long Shadow {{!}} Jacobin|website=www.jacobinmag.com|access-date=2 June 2016}}</ref> The supporters of Célestin protested in the streets, together with the supporters of [[Jean-Charles Moïse]]'s ''Platfom Pitit Desalin'' and supporters of former President [[Jean-Bertrand Aristide]]'s [[Fanmi Lavalas]] party the presidential candidate of which, [[Maryse Narcisse]], finished fourth behind Jean-Charles Moïse and also denounced the results during a news conference. The protesters threw rocks and burned tires. The police responded with tear gas and made some arrests. The police also stopped and searched the vehicle of a former top government prosecutor, Claudy Gassant, who is a supporter of Moïse.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/world/americas/haiti/article43531653.html|title=Célestin disputes Haiti election results; supporters protest|work=miamiherald}}</ref>

Amid allegations of fraud in the 2015 elections, Martelly resigned the presidency on 10 February 2016, leaving Haiti without a president for a week. The National Assembly [[February 2016 Haitian presidential election|elected on 14 February 2016]] [[Jocelerme Privert]] as provisional President.<ref>{{Cite news|title = Haiti Has a President|url = http://www.newyorker.com/news/news-desk/haiti-has-a-president|newspaper = The New Yorker|date = 17 February 2016|issn = 0028-792X|first = Jon Lee|last = Anderson}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title = Haiti interim president vows to hold former leaders accountable|url = https://news.yahoo.com/haiti-interim-president-vows-hold-former-leaders-accountable-051106465.html|website = Yahoo News|access-date = 20 February 2016}}</ref> Privert formed a month-long verification commission to restore legitimacy to the electoral process. In May 2016, the commission audited about 13,000 ballots and determined that the elections had been dishonest and recommended a complete rerun of the election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/world/americas/haiti/article80825277.html|title=Haiti panel calls for re-run of presidential elections|website=miamiherald|access-date=2 June 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/aponline/2016/05/30/world/americas/ap-cb-haiti-election-verification.html|title=Haiti Panel Recommends Throwing Out Results of Disputed Vote|last=The Associated Press|date=30 May 2016|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

On 5 April 2016, the CEP announced that a rerun of the presidential election was to take place on 9 October 2016, alongside the second round of the parliamentary elections that has been suspended and the first round for a third of the Senate.<ref>[http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-17640-haiti-flash-presidential-elections-on-october-9.html Haiti - FLASH : Presidential Elections on October 9]</ref>

==Candidates==
Seventy candidates were initially in the race, including 64 men and six women.<ref name=list>{{cite web|title=LISTE DES CANDIDATS A LA PRESIDENCE AYANT FAIT LEUR DEPOT DE CANDIDATURE|url=http://www.cephaiti.ht/files/Doc_off/candidats_a_la_presidence.pdf|publisher=CONSEIL ELECTORAL PROVISOIRE|access-date=28 May 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150529051714/http://www.cephaiti.ht/files/Doc_off/candidats_a_la_presidence.pdf|archive-date=29 May 2015}}</ref> Two candidates, Mario Andresol and Diony Monestime, ran as independents.<ref name=list/> As of 28 May 2015, 41 challenges had been filed against 23 of the 70 candidates, including Andresol and former Prime Minister [[Laurent Lamothe]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=L|first1=S|title=Haiti - FLASH : 23 Presidential candidates challenged (list)|url=http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-13995-haiti-flash-23-presidential-candidates-challenged-list.html|access-date=28 May 2015|publisher=Haitilibre.com|date=2015-05-26}}</ref> The Departmental Bureau of Electoral Disputes heard those disputes, to determine whether those individuals would be excluded from the presidential race.<ref>{{cite web|title=Haiti: 41 disputes to 23 presidential hopefuls heard at Electoral Bureau|url=http://www.sentinel.ht/news/articles/political/6241-41-disputes-to-23-presidential-hopefuls-heard-at-electoral-bureau|website=Haiti Sentinel|access-date=28 May 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150529051728/http://www.sentinel.ht/news/articles/political/6241-41-disputes-to-23-presidential-hopefuls-heard-at-electoral-bureau|archive-date=29 May 2015}}</ref>

The final list of 58 candidates was published on 12 June,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.haitilibre.com/article-14185-haiti-flash-liste-officielle-des-candidats-agrees-a-la-presidence.html |title=Haïti - FLASH : Liste officielle des candidats agréés à la Présidence |work=Haiti Libre |date=12 June 2015}}</ref> but in the following days two candidates were removed; Jacky Lumarque (''Verité'') and Level Francois (''Parti de la Diaspora Haitienne pour Haiti''), making a new total of 56 candidates.<ref>[http://www.haitilibre.com/en/news-14260-haiti-flash-the-presidential-candidate-of-mudhah-put-aside-because-his-criminal-past.html Haiti - FLASH : The Presidential candidate of MUDHAH put aside because his criminal past]</ref>

==Results==
According to preliminary results posted by the Provisional Electoral Council, Jovenel Moïse obtained 32.81% of the preferences, and Jude Célestin won 25.27%.<ref>[https://electionpresidentielle.cephaiti.ht/rapport_PR/resume-presidentielle.pdf Preliminary Results] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151223052525/https://electionpresidentielle.cephaiti.ht/rapport_PR/resume-presidentielle.pdf |date=2015-12-23 }}</ref> Voter turnout was reportedly 28.8%. 

After the preliminary results were published on 25 October 2015, six candidates disputed the election and denouncing the results as “anti-democratic”. A runoff was initially scheduled for 27 December 2015,<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-election-idUSKCN0SU36N20151105 Haiti's government candidate advances to presidential runoff] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230131001101/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-haiti-election-idUSKCN0SU36N20151105 |date=31 January 2023 }} Reuters, 5 November 2015</ref> but on 22 December the [[Conseil Electoral Provisoire]] announced that the runoff was being postponed indefinitely.<ref name=postpone2/> On 5 April 2016, the CEP announced that a rerun of the presidential election was to take place on 9 October 2016.

