==Aftermath==
Amid declarations of fraud and numerous delays of an elected government, the Provisional President [[Jocelerme Privert|Jocelerme Privet]] created a verification commission in May 2016 to audit the results of the August 2015 legislative elections and first round of the presidential elections.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/aponline/2016/05/30/world/americas/ap-cb-haiti-election-verification.html|title=Haiti Panel Recommends Throwing Out Results of Disputed Vote|last=The Associated Press|date=2016-05-30|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331|access-date=2016-06-01}}</ref> On 30 May 2016 the commission, headed by Pierre François Benoît, issued a report recommending the election be redone citing findings of significant fraud.

While Haiti-based organizations found innumerable counts of fraud and proof of unfair elections on October 25, international observers endorsed the results before the interim government's report had been released.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/22/world/americas/us-presses-for-haiti-runoff-vote-amid-fears-of-violence-and-fraud.html|title=U.S. Presses for Haiti Runoff Vote Amid Fears of Violence and Fraud|last=Robles|first=Frances|date=2016-01-21|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331|access-date=2016-08-08}}</ref> According to the U.S. Deputy Spokesman, for example, "The United States regrets the decision by the Provisional Electoral Council to restart the presidential elections from the first round. This will increase time and resources needed to complete the 2015 electoral process and further delay installation of a constitutionally elected president.... The United States regrets that the electoral process has extended yet again, with the president-elect unlikely to be installed before February 7th, 2017."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://2009-2017.state.gov/r/pa/prs/dpb/2016/06/258228.htm#HAITI|title=Daily Press Briefing - June 8, 2016|access-date=2016-08-08}}</ref> The National Human Rights Defense Network published one of several reports criticizing the happenings on election day, using Haitian observations to bolster claims apparently unseen by many international players.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://rnddh.org/en/october-25th-2015-elections-a-massive-planned-operation-of-electoral-fraud/|title=October 25th, 2015 Elections: A Massive Planned Operation of Electoral Fraud|website=rnddh.org|access-date=2016-08-08|archive-date=2016-08-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160808165542/http://rnddh.org/en/october-25th-2015-elections-a-massive-planned-operation-of-electoral-fraud/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.haitilibre.com/docs/elections2015/declaration-preliminaire-25-oct-2015-MOEUE.pdf|title=Déclaration Préliminaire: Une journée électorale calme et globalement bien organisée dont la participation peine à décoller|last=Union Europeene Mission d'Observation Electorale Haiti 2015|date=27 October 2015|website=Haiti Libre|access-date=8 August 2016}}</ref>

American officials discouraged rerunning the elections after spending $33 million on funding them.<ref>{{Cite web|author=Barry-Shaw, Nikolas|url=https://www.jacobinmag.com/2016/05/haiti-elections-hillary-clinton-fraud-corruption-earthquake-martelly/|title=Clinton's Long Shadow|publisher=Jacobin|website=www.jacobinmag.com|date=27 May 2016|access-date=10 December 2016}}</ref> In an April 2016 visit to Haiti, Special Coordinator [[Kenneth H. Merten]] said that he hoped the verification process would be "very, very fast" and that it would not change the election results.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://cepr.net/blogs/haiti-relief-and-reconstruction-watch/election-verification-results-expected-this-weekend-what-to-expect-and-what-comes-next|title=Election Verification Results Expected this Weekend: What to Expect and What Comes Next?|last=Johnston|first=Jake|website=cepr.net|language=en-US|access-date=2016-06-01|archive-date=2016-05-31|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160531155807/http://cepr.net/blogs/haiti-relief-and-reconstruction-watch/election-verification-results-expected-this-weekend-what-to-expect-and-what-comes-next|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[John Kerry]] also emphasized the need to accept an elected government, citing the lack of international community patience with further delay.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/world/americas/haiti/article72085692.html|title=Kerry's warning to Haiti: Patience is wearing thin on delayed elections|website=miamiherald|access-date=2016-06-01}}</ref> The U.S. Government responded to this report by announcing its refusal to provide funding to complete the Haitian elections.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://2009-2017.state.gov/r/pa/prs/dpb/2016/07/259433.htm#HAITI|title=Daily Press Briefing - July 7, 2016|access-date=2016-08-08}}</ref> This development may prohibit international observers, often funded by the U.S., from observing the next electoral sessions. While the [[Organization of American States|OAS]] received $1 million in U.S. funding for the October 25 election,{{citation needed|date=December 2016}} the organization plans to continue observation, following a list of demanded changes to the Haitian electoral process.<ref>{{Cite web|author=Charles, Jacqueline|date=2 August 2016|url=http://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/world/americas/haiti/article93333377.html|title=OAS will observe Haiti's elections but wants changes ahead of vote|newspaper=Miami Herald|access-date=10 December 2016}}</ref>

